course: biology
lesson: 266
title: The long-necked giants: Diplodocus, Brachiosaurus, Argentinosaurus and Brontosaurus
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The sauropods were the long-necked plant-eaters, the biggest animals that ever walked on land; Diplodocus was about 25 metres long, mostly neck and tail, longer than a tennis court.
explain: The heaviest, like Argentinosaurus, may have weighed 70 tonnes or more, as much as ten elephants, and one of its backbones is taller than a child.
explain: They did not crush themselves because their bones were full of air pockets, like a bird's, which made the skeleton lighter and helped them breathe.
explain: They did not chew at all: they stripped leaves and swallowed them, and their huge gut worked like a compost tank, so lunch for a sauropod lasted most of the day.
explain: Old pictures put Brontosaurus in a lake to hold up its weight, but footprints and leg bones show sauropods walked on dry land, often in groups with the young in the middle.
fact: Sauropods were long-necked plant-eating dinosaurs and the biggest animals ever to walk on land; Diplodocus was about 25 metres long, mostly neck and tail.
fact: Argentinosaurus may have weighed 70 tonnes or more and been about 35 metres long; these sizes are estimates from a few huge bones.
fact: Sauropod bones had air pockets inside, as birds' bones do, which made the skeleton lighter and helped breathing.
fact: Brachiosaurus had long front legs and could reach treetops about 12 metres up; some other sauropods probably held their necks more level, sweeping low plants.
fact: Sauropods did not chew; they stripped leaves, swallowed them whole and let their huge gut break the plants down slowly.
fact: Even the giants hatched from eggs about the size of a football, because a much bigger egg would need a shell too thick for the baby to breathe through; a hatchling of a few kilograms grew thousands of times heavier before it was adult.
fact: Scientists once thought sauropods had to live in lakes to hold up their weight, but footprints and leg bones show they walked on dry land.
fact: In 1903 Brontosaurus was judged to be the same as Apatosaurus, but a 2015 study said it was different enough to be its own kind again.
fact: Trackways show some sauropods walked in groups, with young ones in the middle; footprints are trace fossils that show behaviour.
fact: A sauropod holding its head high needed a big, powerful heart to pump blood all the way up to its head, like a giraffe today; this is one reason some scientists think many held their necks low.
fact: A giraffe has 7 neck bones and a sauropod had 12 to 19; unrelated animals finding the same answer to reaching high food is called convergence.
fact: Only the blue whale, which is held up by water, is heavier than the biggest sauropods.
exercise: What were sauropods? | Long-necked plant-eating dinosaurs | Flying reptiles with long beaks | Meat-eating dinosaurs with big teeth | Sea reptiles with flippers
exercise: About how long was Diplodocus? | About 25 metres | About 2 metres | About 250 metres | About 5 metres
exercise: About how much may Argentinosaurus have weighed? | 70 tonnes or more, like ten elephants | About as much as one big horse, under a tonne | About 1,000 tonnes, as heavy as a big ship | About as much as a family car, one tonne
exercise: What helped a sauropod's skeleton not be too heavy? | Air pockets inside its bones | Bones made of soft rubbery stuff | Having no bones in its neck | Floating in lakes all day
exercise: How high could Brachiosaurus reach? | Treetops about 12 metres up | Only grass at its feet | About 100 metres, above the clouds | About 2 metres, like a horse
exercise: How did sauropods deal with leaves? | They swallowed them without chewing | They chewed each leaf for an hour | They sucked out the juice and spat them out | They stored them in their cheeks
exercise: About how big was a sauropod egg? | About the size of a football | About the size of a small car | About the size of a garden pea | About the size of a bath tub
exercise: Why were giant dinosaur eggs not giant? | A very big egg needs a shell too thick for the baby to breathe | The mothers were too lazy to lay big eggs and chose small ones | Big eggs always rolled away and floated off down the rivers | The babies were born alive, so there were never any eggs at all
exercise: Where did sauropods really live? | On dry land | Only in deep lakes | Only in the sea | Up in the treetops
exercise: What happened to the name Brontosaurus? | It was dropped in 1903 and brought back by a 2015 study | It was always used and never once questioned by any scientist | It was first given to a T. rex and later moved to a sauropod | It was invented for a cartoon and never used by scientists
exercise: What do trackways of sauropods show? | Some walked in groups with the young in the middle | They always walked alone and never met one another | They walked only on their back legs, like a T. rex | They walked backwards to hide which way they went
exercise: Why did a sauropod with its head held high need a big heart? | To pump blood all the way up to its head | To keep its long tail warm on cold nights | To help it float and swim across deep lakes | To push air out for its loud booming roars
exercise: How many neck bones did a sauropod have? | 12 to 19 | Exactly 7, like a giraffe | Only 2 | More than 200
exercise: What is convergence? | Unrelated animals finding the same answer to the same problem | Two animals of the same kind meeting up and living together | Animals changing the colour of their coat when winter comes | Babies growing up to look more and more like their parents
exercise: Which animal is heavier than the biggest sauropods? | The blue whale | The African elephant | T. rex | The hippopotamus
exercise: What kind of fossil is a footprint? | A trace fossil, showing behaviour | A body fossil of a foot | A fossil egg | Not a fossil at all
test: «my book's got Brontosaurus standing in a lake cos it's too heavy for land» What do we know now? | Footprints and leg bones show it walked on dry land | Yes, it was too heavy for land so it lived in water | Yes, it floated about like a boat with its neck up | No, it lived up in the tall trees like a giant sloth
test: What would happen if a sauropod had solid bones all through, with no air pockets? | Its skeleton would be much heavier, perhaps too heavy to stand | It would be lighter and able to lift off the ground and fly | It would shrink down until it was smaller than a dog | Nothing would change, because air pockets weigh the same as bone
test: «giraffe's neck bones vs sauropod's, giraffe has loads more right?» What is right? | No, a giraffe has 7 and a sauropod had 12 to 19 | Yes, a giraffe has about 50 | They both have exactly 7 | Sauropods had no neck bones
test: Count them: 4 Diplodocus walk past in a herd. How many legs, heads and tails is that? | 16 legs, 4 heads and 4 tails | 8 legs, 4 heads and 4 tails | 16 legs, 8 heads and 4 tails | 16 legs, 4 heads and 8 tails
test: In a cartoon a dinosaur chews its leaves slowly like a cow. What did real sauropods do? | Swallowed leaves whole and let the gut break them down | Chewed every leaf many times with big flat teeth, like a cow | Ate only stones and never touched a single leaf | Licked the juice off the leaves and left the rest
test: «Bronto's not real, my dad says, it's just Apatosaurus» Who is right? | A 2015 study said Brontosaurus is its own kind again, so the name is back | Dad, the name was dropped for ever | Neither, both names are made up | Brontosaurus is a kind of whale
test: A baby sauropod hatches from an egg the size of a football. What happens next? | It grows thousands of times heavier before it is adult | It stays the size of a football for all of its life | It changes into a bird after its first year | It grows to full adult size in just one week
test: Why might some scientists think many sauropods kept their necks fairly level? | Lifting the head high makes the heart work very hard | Their necks were far too short to lift up high at all | They were always bending down to look for water to drink | They were afraid of the birds flying above the trees
test: A giraffe and a sauropod both have long necks for high food, but they are not related. What is this called? | Convergence | Extinction | Hibernation | Migration
test: «how does a huge thing like that breathe properly» What helped sauropods breathe? | Air sacs linked to the bones, like a bird's | A big blowhole on top of the head, like a whale | Gills along the neck, like a fish | Breathing only through the skin, like a frog
test: «if Argentinosaurus is the heaviest thing ever, beat that» What is true? | The blue whale is heavier, held up by water | Yes, nothing has ever been heavier | A T. rex was heavier | An elephant is heavier
exam: Identify the feature of the sauropod skeleton that is understood to have reduced its overall mass. | The presence of air-filled spaces within the bones | The complete absence of any bones within the neck | The composition of the bones from cartilage alone | The replacement of the heavy tail by a fin of skin
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the habitat of sauropods. | Trackways and limb bones demonstrate that they walked upon dry land | They are understood to have dwelt exclusively in deep lakes | They are understood to have inhabited the open ocean | They are understood to have lived principally in trees
exam: Ascertain the manner in which sauropods processed the vegetation upon which they fed. | It was swallowed without chewing and broken down slowly within the gut | It was chewed at great length by flattened molars | It was rejected after the extraction of its juices | It was stored in pouches within the cheeks
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the phenomenon termed convergence? | The independent arrival of unrelated organisms at similar solutions to similar problems | The descent of all organisms of one kind from a single parent within one generation | The seasonal movement of animals over great distances in search of food | The resemblance of offspring to their parents in appearance and habit
